    id: "transport-layer", title: "Transport Layer", difficulty: "medium",
    definition: "The transport layer provides end-to-end communication between processes (not just hosts) using port numbers. Two main protocols: TCP (reliable, ordered, connection-oriented — for web, email, file transfer) and UDP (unreliable, unordered, connectionless — for DNS, video streaming, gaming). The transport layer handles segmentation, flow control, error recovery, and multiplexing (multiple processes sharing one IP via ports). It's the process-to-process layer.",
    coreUnderstanding: "The transport layer (OSI layer 4 / TCP/IP Transport) provides end-to-end communication between processes, not just hosts. While the network layer (IP) delivers to a host, the transport layer delivers to a specific process on that host using port numbers. Two main protocols: TCP (reliable, ordered, connection-oriented — used for web, email, file transfer where reliability matters) and UDP (unreliable, unordered, connectionless — used for DNS, video streaming, gaming where speed matters). The transport layer handles segmentation (breaking data into segments), flow control (don't overwhelm the receiver), error recovery (retransmit lost segments), and multiplexing (multiple processes share one IP via ports).",
    howItWorks: "1. Process-to-process delivery: the network layer delivers to a host (IP address); the transport layer delivers to a process (port number).\n2. Port numbers: 16-bit, 0-65535. Well-known ports (0-1023): HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, DNS 53, SMTP 25. Registered (1024-49151). Dynamic (49152-65535).\n3. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): reliable, ordered, connection-oriented. 3-way handshake, sequence numbers, ACKs, retransmission, flow control, congestion control.\n4. UDP (User Datagram Protocol): unreliable, unordered, connectionless. No setup, no guarantees, no flow control. Fast, low overhead.\n5. Segmentation: data is broken into segments. Each segment has a transport header (port numbers, sequence numbers for TCP).\n6. Multiplexing: multiple processes share one IP via different ports. Demultiplexing: the receiver delivers each segment to the correct process based on the port.\n7. Flow control: prevent overwhelming the receiver (sliding window). Congestion control: prevent overwhelming the network.",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "bash", code: "# View active connections (transport layer)\nnetstat -tlnp  # TCP listening\n# Proto Local Address     Foreign Address  State\n# tcp   0.0.0.0:80        0.0.0.0:*        LISTEN  (web server)\n# tcp   0.0.0.0:443       0.0.0.0:*        LISTEN  (HTTPS)\n# tcp   0.0.0.0:22        0.0.0.0:*        LISTEN  (SSH)\n# udp   0.0.0.0:53        0.0.0.0:*                (DNS)\n\n# Well-known ports:\n# HTTP: 80    HTTPS: 443   DNS: 53\n# SMTP: 25    FTP: 21      SSH: 22\n# POP3: 110   IMAP: 143\n\n# TCP vs UDP:\n# TCP: reliable, ordered, connection-oriented\n#   - Web (HTTP), email (SMTP), file transfer (FTP), SSH\n#   - 3-way handshake, ACKs, retransmission\n# UDP: fast, unreliable, connectionless\n#   - DNS, video streaming, gaming, DHCP\n#   - No setup, no guarantees\n\n# Multiplexing: multiple processes share one IP\n# Process A: 192.168.1.1:80  (web server)\n# Process B: 192.168.1.1:22  (SSH)\n# Process C: 192.168.1.1:53  (DNS)\n# All on the same IP, different ports", output: "-- Transport: process-to-process delivery via ports.\n-- TCP: reliable, ordered (web, email, files).\n-- UDP: fast, unreliable (DNS, streaming, gaming).", note: "The transport layer delivers to processes via port numbers. TCP is reliable (web, email); UDP is fast (DNS, streaming). Multiplexing: multiple processes share one IP via ports." }],
    marginNotes: ["The transport layer is process-to-process (ports), while the network layer is host-to-host (IP addresses). Ports identify which process on the host.", "Well-known ports (0-1023): HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, DNS 53, SMTP 25, SSH 22, FTP 21. These are standardized.", "TCP is for reliability (web, email, files); UDP is for speed (DNS, streaming, gaming). Choose based on whether reliability or speed matters more."],
